.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Spin a disk and set it rolling flat on a table and it settles into a shuddering near-steady state described, at each instant, by just its inclination angle \theta (between the disk plane and the table) and precession angle \phi (the azimuthal angle of the rolling contact point). Its contact point precesses faster and faster -- the characteristic rattle rising in pitch -- right up until the disk suddenly stops: a finite-time singularity. Rather than committing to one first-principles dissipation mechanism (viscous air drag vs. rolling friction are both argued for in the literature; see the history page), EulersDisk uses a reduced phenomenological pair of ODEs,">

